<div class="space-y-4">
<details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
<summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">What is the most common type of mouth infection?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary>
<div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Odontogenic (tooth-origin) infections are the most common mouth infections seen in clinical practice. These begin as dental decay that progresses to infect the tooth pulp, then spreads to the root apex and surrounding bone. The most common presenting sign is a dental abscess with gum swelling. <a href="dental-abscess-treatment-hyderabad.php" class="text-secondary underline">Dental abscess treatment</a> covers this in detail.</div>
</details>
<details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
<summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">Can mouth infections spread to other parts of the body?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary>
<div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Yes — mouth infections can spread locally into facial spaces (causing facial swelling), spread hematogenously (through the bloodstream) to affect the heart (endocarditis), joints (septic arthritis), or sinuses. In severe cases, a mouth abscess can cause Ludwig's angina (floor-of-mouth infection that obstructs the airway). This is why mouth infections should never be ignored.</div>
</details>
<details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
<summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">Will antibiotics alone cure my mouth infection?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary>
<div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">No — for tooth-origin (odontogenic) infections, antibiotics alone provide temporary relief. The source of the infection (the tooth) remains, and the infection recurs once antibiotics are stopped. Definitive treatment requires addressing the source: either root canal therapy to save the tooth, or extraction to remove it. Our surgeons always discuss tooth-preservation options before recommending extraction.</div>
</details>
</div>
</div>
